---
description: Data sources, projections, SQL templates, and filter placeholders
globs: dashboards/**/*.yaml
alwaysApply: false
---

<rule>
<meta>
  This rule covers datasource definitions for A7S Skipper dashboards: ClickHouse sources, views, logical/materialized projections, skills, filter macros, freshness, and platform datasources.
  Activate when writing or editing `datasources:` blocks, `_shared/_datasources.yaml`, or widget `datasource:` references.
</meta>

<concepts>
  <data_flow>ClickHouse/skill SQL → Parquet → DuckDB base table → projections/views → widget queries DuckDB locally</data_flow>
  <sql_dialects>
    - **Base datasource SQL** → ClickHouse syntax
    - **Projection, view, inline widget SQL** → DuckDB syntax
    - **Notebook projections** → `sql:` cells run in DuckDB; `code:` cells run Python and return tabular output. Tab `runtime: client | server | remote` decides execution target.
    - Never mix dialects. Base sources talk to ClickHouse; everything else is local DuckDB.
  </sql_dialects>
  <datasource_types>
    | Type | `type` field | SQL dialect | Data flow |
    |---|---|---|---|
    | ClickHouse | omitted | ClickHouse | Remote fetch → Parquet → DuckDB |
    | View | `view` | DuckDB | Local DuckDB VIEW from `{{source:*}}` refs |
    | Skill | `skill` | dot-notation | Server-side API read or guarded write skill |
    | Platform | `_` prefix key | — | Internal store (MongoDB) → Parquet → DuckDB |
  </datasource_types>
</concepts>

<schema>
  <datasource_definition>
    ```yaml
    datasources:
      total_by_ad:                          # Key used by widgets
        source: default                     # Optional. Registry key (default: "default")
        sql: |                              # Required. ClickHouse SQL
          SELECT install_date, media_source, costs, revenue_net
          FROM marts.marketing_creatives
          WHERE ${filters.date_range('toDate(install_date)')}
        projections: {}                     # Optional. Named DuckDB sub-queries
        default_projection: daily_costs     # Optional. Default for widgets
        cache_ttl: 300                      # Optional. Skill cache TTL in seconds
        input: []                           # Optional. Skill user input fields
        activity:                           # Optional. Safe params copied to activity
          params: [business_key]
        post_actions: []                    # Optional. Post-write hooks
        post_each_actions: []               # Optional. Per-entity post-write hooks
        ai_hint: "..."                      # Optional. AI context (string or string[])
        freshness: {}                       # Optional. Watermark tracking config
    ```
  </datasource_definition>

  <projection>
    ```yaml
    projections:
      daily_costs_by_source:
        description: Daily costs by media source     # Optional
        ai_hint: "Best for trend analysis"           # Optional
        materialized: true                          # Optional. Physical DuckDB revision table + stable logical view
        sql: |                                       # DuckDB syntax
          SELECT
            CAST(install_date AS DATE) as date,
            media_source,
            SUM(costs) as costs
          FROM {{source:total_by_ad}}
          GROUP BY 1, 2
          ORDER BY 1 DESC
        columns:                                     # Optional. Metadata for AI and filters
          date: { type: date, description: Date of install }
          media_source: { type: string, description: "Media source", mapping: media_sources }
          costs: { type: decimal, description: Total spend, unit: USD }
    ```
  </projection>

  <notebook_code_projection>
    Projections are notebook cells when the tab uses `mode: notebook`. Name them uniquely within the notebook root to avoid persisted parquet collisions.

    ```yaml
    projections:
      nb_top_campaigns:
        sql: |
          SELECT campaign, SUM(costs) AS costs, SUM(revenue) AS revenue
          FROM {{source:marketing}}
          GROUP BY campaign
          ORDER BY revenue DESC
          LIMIT 25

      nb_stats_summary:
        code: |
          import pandas as pd
          df = input_table
          result = pd.DataFrame([{"rows": len(df)}])
    ```

    Successful runs materialize DuckDB tables and persist parquet outputs via `/api/notebook/cell`. Widgets should point at the projection key (`datasource: nb_top_campaigns`) after the cell has run.
  </notebook_code_projection>

  <projection_column_types>
    `type: string | decimal | integer | timestamp | boolean | date`
    Optional fields: `unit`, `mapping` (reference to dashboard mapping), `values` (known values), `pii` (boolean), `ai_hint`
  </projection_column_types>

  <materialized_projection>
    Use `materialized: true` when a rich_table is backed by a projection over a write-skill/base source and must refresh immediately after optimistic writes, rollback, confirmation, discard, errors, or refetch.

    ```yaml
    datasources:
      fb_campaigns:
        type: skill
        sql: |
          SELECT id, name, status, daily_budget
          FROM fb.act_123.getCampaigns
        projections:
          fb_campaigns_table:
            materialized: true
            sql: |
              SELECT id, name, status, daily_budget
              FROM {{source:fb_campaigns}}
    sections:
      - id: campaigns
        widgets:
          - id: campaigns-table
            type: rich_table
            datasource: fb_campaigns_table
    ```

    Materialized projections still use DuckDB syntax. Runtime creates immutable physical revision tables, swaps a stable logical view, and tracks the projection with its own cascade-store key. Existing rows remain visible while refresh recomputes.
  </materialized_projection>

  <view_datasource>
    ```yaml
    daily_combined:
      type: view                              # Required for views
      sql: |                                  # DuckDB syntax
        WITH ad_daily AS (
          SELECT CAST(install_date AS DATE) AS date, SUM(costs) AS ad_costs
          FROM {{source:total_by_ad}}
          GROUP BY 1
        ),
        upsale_daily AS (
          SELECT day AS date, SUM(revenue_net) AS upsale_revenue
          FROM {{source:upsales_by_day}}
          GROUP BY 1
        )
        SELECT
          COALESCE(a.date, u.date) AS date,
          COALESCE(a.ad_costs, 0) AS ad_costs,
          COALESCE(u.upsale_revenue, 0) AS upsale_revenue
        FROM ad_daily a
        FULL OUTER JOIN upsale_daily u ON a.date = u.date
    ```
  </view_datasource>

  <inline_widget_datasource>
    All widget types support inline `datasource: { sql }`. The loader auto-registers it as a `type: view` with key `{widget.id}__view`.

    ```yaml
    - id: revenue-chart
      type: chart
      datasource:
        sql: |
          SELECT date, SUM(revenue) as revenue
          FROM {{source:orders}}
          GROUP BY date
    ```
  </inline_widget_datasource>
</schema>

<options>
  <filter_macros>
    Use `${filters.<id>}` or `${filters.<id>('column_expr')}` in SQL:

    | Syntax | When set | When empty/null |
    |---|---|---|
    | `${filters.id('col')}` | `col IN ('v')` | `1=1` |
    | `${filters.id('col').if_set}` | `AND col IN ('v')` | `` (empty string) |
    | `${filters.id('col').or_true}` | `col IN ('v')` | `1=1` |
    | `${filters.id('col').required}` | `AND col IN ('v')` | `AND 1=0` |
    | `${filters.id('col').or_false}` | `col IN ('v')` | `1=0` |
    | `${filters.id.value}` | `'v'` (SQL-quoted) | `'NULL'` |
    | `${filters.id.values}` | `['a', 'b']` | `[]` |
    | `${filters.id.sql}` | `col IN ('v')` (bare) | `1=1` |
    | `${filters.id.start}` | `'2024-01-01'` (date_range) | `NULL` |
    | `${filters.id.end}` | `'2024-01-31'` (date_range) | `NULL` |
    | `${filters.id.days}` | `30` (date_range) | `NULL` |

    **Default** (`${filters.id}`): write `AND` manually. Use with `WHERE 1=1 AND ${filters.id}`.
    **`.if_set`**: includes `AND` automatically — use WITHOUT manual `AND`. Best for optional/cascading filters.
    **`.required` / `.or_false`**: inverse of `.if_set` / `.or_true` — when the filter is empty, render `AND 1=0` / `1=0` to force zero rows. Use to gate heavy queries until the user picks a value instead of the `clearable: false + default` workaround.
  </filter_macros>

  <required_filters title="Gating heavy queries on filter selection">
    Use `.required` to avoid scanning a whole table before the user picks a filter value:

    ```sql
    SELECT *
    FROM marts.events
    WHERE 1=1
      ${filters.date_range('toDate(ts)').if_set}
      ${filters.account_id('account_id').required}
    ```

    - Empty `account_id` → `AND 1=0` → query returns zero rows instantly (nothing fetched from ClickHouse).
    - As soon as a value is selected → `AND account_id IN (...)` kicks in.

    Use `.or_false` (no `AND` prefix) when the condition is the only thing in `WHERE`:
    `WHERE ${filters.account_id('account_id').or_false}`.

    Preferred over `clearable: false + default` because it does not silently ship a pre-selected value to every viewer.
  </required_filters>

  <auto_filters>
    | Syntax | Description |
    |---|---|
    | `${filters.auto}` | All non-empty filters combined with AND |
    | `${filters.auto.except('a,b')}` | All except listed filter IDs |
    | `${filters.auto.only('a,b')}` | Only listed filter IDs |

    ```sql
    WHERE 1=1 AND ${filters.auto}
    WHERE 1=1 AND ${filters.auto.except('granularity')}
    ```
  </auto_filters>

  <selection_macros>
    Projections can reference widget row selections via `${selection.<widget_id>}`:

    | Macro | Selected | Empty |
    |---|---|---|
    | `${selection.widget('col')}` | `AND col IN ('a','b')` | `AND 1=1` |
    | `${selection.widget('col').if_set}` | `AND col IN (...)` | `` (empty) |
    | `${selection.widget('col').value}` | `'first_val'` | `NULL` |
    | `${selection.widget('col').values}` | `'a', 'b'` | `` (empty) |

    The source widget must have `selectable` configured.
  </selection_macros>

  <post_actions>
    Declarative hooks after write skill execution. Standard rich_table write actions do not need local optimistic SQL: the client infers strict local UPDATEs from the write skill SQL, row params, and datasource descriptor. Keep `post_actions` focused on server cache invalidation and explicit refetch.

    | Type | Where | Effect |
    |---|---|---|
    | `invalidate: <key>` | Server + client | Clears server cache; marks source for re-fetch |
    | `refresh: <key>` | Client | Forces full re-fetch of a datasource |
    | `sql: <stmt>` | Client DuckDB | Legacy fallback only. Prefer inferred optimistic updates + materialized projections. |

    ```yaml
    post_actions:
      - invalidate: fb_campaigns
      - sql: UPDATE {{source:fb_campaigns}} SET daily_budget = {new_budget} WHERE id = {id}
    ```
  </post_actions>

  <sql_select_skill_input>
    A write skill may request one or more independent SQL-backed single-select values. Each input
    submits one scalar; this does not enable multi-select or bulk execution. Its options query runs
    locally in DuckDB when the action dialog opens, not as a remote datasource query. Keep each query
    to one read-only `SELECT` statement and reference dependencies through `{{source:key}}`. Platform
    dependencies such as `{{source:_platform.v1.activity_log.24h}}` are resolved into the skill
    descriptor like any other dependency.

    ```yaml
    input:
      - name: target_status
        type: select
        label: Select campaign status
        required: true
        options:
          sql: |
            SELECT
              value,                         -- required submitted scalar
              value_title,                   -- optional primary option text
              'Campaign status' AS value_label,
              NULL AS value_meta,
              (value = {status}) AS selected,
              FALSE AS disabled,
              FALSE AS completed
            FROM (VALUES
              ('ACTIVE', 'Active', 1),
              ('PAUSED', 'Paused', 2)
            ) AS statuses(value, value_title, sort_order)
            ORDER BY sort_order
    ```

    Option columns:
    - `value` is required and becomes the flat write param (`{target_status}` above).
    - `value_title`, `value_label`, and `value_meta` are optional presentation strings.
    - `selected`, `disabled`, and `completed` accept booleans or `0`/`1`.
    - `selected` is only the initial/current-value hint. It does not disable or complete an option,
      and the current user choice lives in React after the dialog opens.
    - If exactly one available row has `selected = 1`, it is the initial choice. Otherwise the first
      available row in deterministic SQL order is selected. Completed or disabled rows are never
      available.

    Options SQL is executed through prepared statements for scalar row placeholders. Do not concatenate URLs,
    credentials, tokens, or other secrets into the query.
  </sql_select_skill_input>

  <write_skill_activity>
    `activity.params` is an allowlist for safe business correlation fields copied to successful action
    activity. Use only fields needed by board SQL, for example `[ad_id, target_account_id]`; never include
    credentials, tokens, authorization headers, or unrestricted payloads. The same allowlist/redaction
    projection is applied to action-log and execution diagnostics.

    A board may overlay recent successful activity on delayed history, but it must compute state as a
    SQL `UNION` with business-key deduplication. Do not add a Skipper-owned ledger, queue, reservation,
    or durable operation state. For upload state, deduplicate by `(ad_id, target_account_id)` and refresh
    both the platform activity datasource and the dependent local view after success.
  </write_skill_activity>
</options>

<examples>
  <cascading_filters>
    ```sql
    SELECT DISTINCT language_level AS value, language_level AS label
    FROM {{source:content_list}}
    WHERE 1=1
      ${filters.platform('platform').if_set}
      ${filters.native_language('native_language').if_set}
    ```
  </cascading_filters>

  <skill_read>
    ```yaml
    fb_campaigns:
      type: skill
      sql: |
        SELECT id, name, status FROM fb.act_123.getCampaigns
        WHERE "time_range.since" = ${filters.date_range.start}
        LIMIT 100
      cache_ttl: 300
    ```
  </skill_read>

  <skill_write>
    ```yaml
    pause_campaign:
      type: skill
      sql: |
        WITH error__target_status_allowed AS (
          SELECT 1
          FROM (VALUES ('ACTIVE'), ('PAUSED')) AS allowed(value)
          WHERE value = '{target_status}'
        ),
        discard__same_status AS (
          SELECT 'Campaign already has this status' AS message
          WHERE '{status}' = '{target_status}'
        )
        UPDATE fb.{id}.updateCampaign
        SET status = {target_status}
        WHERE campaign_id = {id}
      input:
        - name: target_status
          type: select
          label: Select campaign status
          required: true
          options:
            sql: |
              SELECT value, value AS value_title, (value = {status}) AS selected,
                     FALSE AS disabled, FALSE AS completed
              FROM (VALUES ('ACTIVE', 1), ('PAUSED', 2)) AS statuses(value, sort_order)
              ORDER BY sort_order
    ```

    CTE verdict convention:
    | Prefix | Trigger | Result |
    |---|---|---|
    | `error__<name>` | Returns empty | ERROR (blocks write) |
    | `warn__<name>` | Returns rows | WARN (proceeds) |
    | `confirm__<name>` | Returns rows | CONFIRM (requires re-send) |
    | `discard__<name>` | Returns rows | DISCARD (no-op) |
  </skill_write>

  <skill_single_row_insert>
    Write skills also support one explicit-column `INSERT INTO ... (columns) VALUES (...)` statement.
    Multi-row `VALUES`, `INSERT ... SELECT`, bulk jobs, and client-side media transport are not supported.

    ```yaml
    upload_meta_video:
      type: skill
      activity:
        params: [ad_id, target_account_id]
      sql: |
        INSERT INTO fb.{target_account_id}.uploadAdVideo (source, title)
        VALUES ({media_url}, {creative_name})
      post_actions:
        - refresh: _platform.v1.activity_log.24h
        - refresh: creative_upload_state__view
    ```

    `source` is a logical directly downloadable HTTPS URL prepared only in server-side execution and
    removed before base64/multipart encoding. Credentials and host allowlists belong only in the
    trusted server source registry/environment, never dashboard authoring YAML or client params.
    `uploadAdImage`/`uploadAdVideo` use the normal server HTTP executor. The default local/compose source
    configuration resolves `FB_API_BASE_URL` and `FB_ACCESS_TOKEN` to the MockServer URL and dedicated
    mock token so image base64 and streaming multipart can be exercised without real credentials.
  </skill_single_row_insert>

  <shared_datasources>
    ```yaml
    # _shared/_datasources.yaml — flat dict, merged into dashboard datasources
    orders_main:
      sql: |
        SELECT * FROM marts.orders
        WHERE ${filters.date_range('toDate(created_at)')}
    ```

    Merge hierarchy: Dashboard `_shared/` is base → tab-content files extend/override → tab projections extend.
  </shared_datasources>

  <widget_datasource_references>
    ```yaml
    # By key — base source or view
    - id: my_table
      type: rich_table
      datasource: total_by_ad

    # By projection name
    - id: my_chart
      type: chart
      datasource: daily_costs_by_source

    # Inline SQL (auto-creates view)
    - id: my_summary
      type: markdown
      datasource:
        sql: SELECT SUM(costs) AS total_costs FROM {{source:total_by_ad}}
    ```
  </widget_datasource_references>

  <naming_for_presets>
    For rich_table data presets, prefer explicit stable names:
    - `<table_id>__default_view` — default startup projection
    - `<table_id>__view_<preset_id>` — system preset projections

    Inline preset SQL can use `{{source:__default_view}}` to reference the widget's own datasource.
  </naming_for_presets>
</examples>

<patterns>
  <freshness>
    Dashboard-level:
    ```yaml
    freshness:
      enabled: true
      thresholds: { fresh: "5m", stale: "30m", critical: "2h" }
      sources:
        orders_main:
          watermark_column: updated_at
          thresholds: { fresh: "10m", stale: "1h" }
    ```

    Per-datasource: `freshness: false` or `freshness: { enabled: false }` to skip tracking.
    `freshness: false` at dashboard level hides indicators, keeps reload button.

    Threshold cascade: hardcoded defaults → dashboard → source.
    Tracked: non-view, non-skill datasources by default.
  </freshness>

  <platform_datasources>
    Platform sources are built-in, prefixed with `_`. No `datasources:` definition needed.

    Naming: `_platform.v<N>.<source>[.<param>]`
    Available: `_platform.v1.activity_log`, `.24h`, `.7d`, `.30d`
    Legacy alias: `_activity_log` → `_platform.v1.activity_log`

    ```yaml
    datasource: _platform.v1.activity_log.7d
    ```

    Platform sources are auto-discovered by the descriptor resolver from:
    - `{{source:_...}}` references in datasource/projection/view SQL
    - Widget `datasource:` fields with `_`-prefixed keys

    They receive full descriptors (tableName, storeKey) just like explicit datasources.
  </platform_datasources>

  <view_rules>
    - `type: view` required to distinguish from ClickHouse sources
    - SQL uses DuckDB syntax
    - `{{source:*}}` refs must point to base sources only — **view-to-view deps are forbidden**
    - `${filters.*}` supported for DuckDB-side filtering
    - Views can have their own projections
  </view_rules>
</patterns>

<rules>
  <hard_constraints>
    - Widgets MUST NOT make remote calls — all data from local DuckDB
    - NEVER hardcode table names in widget SQL — reference datasources via {{source:key}}.
    - NEVER hardcode date ranges — use ${filters.*} macros so dashboard filters reach the SQL.
    - NEVER SUM pre-computed ratios (ROI, CTR, CPC, ARPU) — re-derive them from components.
    - Always guard division by zero: NULLIF(denominator, 0).
    - Base SQL = ClickHouse syntax; projection/view/inline SQL = DuckDB syntax
    - Datasource access is derived from RBAC grants on the owning dashboard path
    - Inline widget datasource accepts `sql` and optional `materialized: true` for rich_table; no `type`, `source`, or `projections`
    - SQL-backed skill inputs are single-select only; options SQL is one local read-only SELECT
    - Write INSERT support is one row only: `INSERT INTO ... (columns) VALUES (...)`
    - Upload transport and credentials come only from trusted source configuration; never dashboard authoring
  </hard_constraints>
</rules>

